# rules: kotlin-webview-file-access-from-urls
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-200.kt
# from: CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information), CWE-668
#
# Both of these are false by default and both of them turn off the same-origin
# policy for the origin that every local file shares. With either on, a page
# opened from `file://` - and that includes anything an attacker can get into
# the download directory or onto external storage - can read the app's own
# private files with XMLHttpRequest and post them anywhere.
#
# `allowFileAccess` is deliberately not here. It was true by default until API
# 30, so a program that sets it is usually a program restating the default, and
# reporting those would bury the two switches that are actually a decision.
#
# Two spellings each, because Kotlin sees the Java setter and the synthesised
# property and a program will use either. The property assignment has to be
# written with the whole chain - `$V.settings.x = true` rather than
# `$S.x = true` - because a Kotlin assignment target is a flat run of
# navigation suffixes rather than a nesting, so a hole standing for `v.settings`
# has nothing to bind to. The second spelling in each pair covers the
# `val settings = view.settings` case that the first one cannot reach.

["$V.settings.allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs = true",
 "$S.allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs = true",
 "$V.settings.allowFileAccessFromFileURLs = true",
 "$S.allowFileAccessFromFileURLs = true",
 "$S.setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs(true)",
 "$S.setAllowFileAccessFromFileURLs(true)"] as $opened
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $opened) as $all
| ($all | of($opened))
| finding("kotlin-webview-file-access-from-urls";
    "this lets a page loaded from file:// read every other file the app can, "
    + "and a downloaded file is such a page")
| report
